Pushed two follow-up commits resolving the effort-level inconsistencies flagged above.

**`ollama-cloud` was wrong, but not in the direction I assumed.** I researched Ollama's OpenAI-compatible surface: `reasoning_effort` was documented in v0.18.0 (ollama/ollama#14820, ollama/ollama#14821, merged March 2026). The source in `openai/openai.go` validates it against `high/medium/low/max/none`, **rejects anything else with an error**, and passes the graded value through as a string rather than collapsing it to a boolean:

```go
if !slices.Contains([]string{"high", "medium", "low", "max", "none"}, effort) {
    return nil, fmt.Errorf("invalid reasoning value: '%s'...", effort)
}
if effort == "none" { think = &api.ThinkValue{Value: false} } else { think = &api.ThinkValue{Value: effort} }
```

So the forwarder's "Ollama does not document reasoning_effort" comment was stale, and it was silently discarding every effort selection — Ollama Cloud turns always ran at the model default. Fixed by forwarding the value through a mapping into Ollama's accepted set (Codex rungs like `xhigh` and `minimal` would 400 if passed verbatim), and widening DeepSeek V4 Pro and GLM-5.2 to the `high/max` ladder their other resellers already offer. Kimi K2.7 Code and MiniMax M3 keep a single tier, which already matched their peers.

**`qwen-plan/glm-5.2` defaulted to `high`** where Z.ai, opencode-go, and zai-coding all use `max`. Because the qwen-plan profile always sends `reasoning_effort` explicitly, that default was what reached DashScope — anyone picking GLM-5.2 on Qwen Plan silently got the weaker tier the same model serves at max everywhere else.

Two regression tests lock both classes of bug: resellers of one upstream model must agree on `defaultEffort` when their ladders match, and Ollama Cloud models may only advertise levels Ollama accepts. A re-audit now reports no cross-provider effort inconsistencies anywhere in the registry. Suite: 336 pass, 0 fail.

One caveat: no Ollama Cloud credentials on this machine, so the forwarding change is covered by the subprocess forwarder test rather than a live call.

Reviewed this closely. The security-critical parts are genuinely well built, and I want to say what I verified rather than just what's wrong:

- No credential leakage: `resolveProviderCredential` returns `value: undefined` for anonymous providers, and `upstreamHeaders` explicitly skips `Authorization`/`x-api-key`, so the gateway's internal bearer never goes upstream.
- `ANONYMOUS_ENDPOINTS` is real enforcement, not documentation — hardcoded allowlist, `baseUrlEnv` override forbidden, `credential`/`keyless` metadata forbidden, `anonymousModelPolicy`/`anonymousNote` required.
- The free-model policy is enforced at **both** registry load and the user-curation overlay, closing the "curate a paid model id through the free route" loophole end to end.
- Support-bundle redaction correctly skips anonymous providers.

One blocking issue, and it isn't where it looks.

### Anonymous providers are enabled without consent

`src/provider-selection.mjs:90` and `src/setup.mjs:172` treat `authMode === "anonymous"` identically to `keyless`. But `keyless` is loopback-enforced — nothing leaves the machine. `anonymous` means the prompt, and whatever was pasted into it, reaches `console.opencode.ai` / `api.kilo.ai`. Result: both gateways arrive **pre-checked** in the guided picker, and a bare `./install.sh --auto` writes them into `enabled-providers.json` with no prompt at all. Your own test locks this in — zero credentials gives `["kilo-free", "local", "opencode-free"]`.

**The obvious fix doesn't work.** Reverting the `|| provider.authMode === "anonymous"` clause leaves the behavior unchanged, because this PR also changed `resolveProviderCredential()` to return a persistent marker for anonymous providers — so the `credentialStatus(provider, { persistent: true }).configured` fallback branch returns `true` anyway. The exclusion has to be explicit.

The underlying problem is that `configuredProviderIds()` answers two different questions that diverge for exactly one class:

| Question | Callers | Anonymous included? |
|---|---|---|
| "can this authenticate?" | `ensure-configured` gate, `catalog.mjs`, `selectedConfiguredListedModels()` | **yes** — otherwise someone who explicitly chose one is told to run setup and their curated models vanish |
| "may this be on when nobody asked?" | `bin/install` → `ensure-configured` no-file branch, `--providers configured`, non-guided setup | **no** |

So: add a second export rather than changing the first.

```js
// Which providers a run that was told nothing may switch on by itself.
// `configuredProviderIds()` answers "can this authenticate"; this answers "may
// this be turned on when nobody asked for it". They diverge for exactly one
// class. An anonymous provider needs no credential, so the first question says
// yes on a machine that has never heard of it — but unlike `local`, which
// serves from this machine, enabling it points the operator's prompts at a
// third party over the internet.
export function defaultProviderIds() {
  return configuredProviderIds().filter(
    (id) => PROVIDERS.get(id)?.authMode !== "anonymous",
  );
}
```

Then swap `configuredProviderIds()` → `defaultProviderIds()` at the three write sites (`enableProvider`, `disableProvider`, `ensure-configured`) and in `setup.mjs` / `setup-shared.mjs` `selectProviders()` — both the `"configured"` branch and the no-flag default. Leave `providerConfigured()` and `providers.mjs` `configured()` alone, or an explicitly-selected anonymous provider fails the install with a credential prompt.

Separately, the pre-checked box has its own cause — and **your own code already shows the intended design**. You added `anonymous: "no API key"` to `ACTION_LABELS` in `src/setup-ui.mjs` and then never emit it. `provider-onboarding.mjs` sets `entry.action = "ready"`, and the guided picker pre-checks every `ready` row:

```diff
         entry.kind = "anonymous";
-        entry.action = "ready";
+        // Not "ready": the guided picker pre-checks every `ready` row, and an
+        // internet endpoint must be chosen rather than arrive checked.
+        entry.action = "anonymous";
```

Nothing else consumes `action` — the desktop UI and Swift tray key off `kind`/`configured`/`anonymousNote` — so this is contained.

Tests: `test/provider-selection.test.mjs:58,61` keep their current values (they're the right answer to "can authenticate") and gain `defaultProviderIds()` assertions; `test/setup.test.mjs:50,52` revert to `["deepseek", "local"]`; `test/provider-onboarding.test.mjs:83` becomes `"anonymous"`. Worth adding the regression that would have caught this: `ensure-configured` with no selection file — exactly what `bin/install` runs — must write `["deepseek", "local"]`.

I applied this whole patch to a scratch copy of your tree and ran the suite: 1023 pass, plus `scripts-check`. The one failure (`test/skills-install.test.mjs:55`) fails identically on your unpatched branch — pre-existing, unrelated.

**Severity context, in fairness:** because both gateways ship zero preselected models, an auto-enabled provider produces no traffic until someone runs `bin/curate-models`. So this is not live exfiltration today. It's still an endpoint the operator never chose being written into their config and shown as connected — and the first curated model turns it into traffic.

### AGENTS.md

`AGENTS.md` is the canonical installation contract for both agent families and this PR doesn't touch it, so an installing agent has no way to learn these providers exist — and per the above, omitting them from `--providers` doesn't actually withhold them. Needs: the two ids added to the step-4 selectable list with an explicit "never select one on the user's behalf", and a short section defining `authMode: "anonymous"` against `keyless` (off-box vs on-box, configured-but-never-default, catalog-only). I'd rather write that wording myself since it's the shared contract — I'll follow up, you don't need to.

Everything else here I'd merge happily. Fix the consent path and this is good to un-draft.

