## Your review was right — the bound came from it, not before it

Please don't retract that one; the round trip wasn't wasted.

The commit you reviewed was `e45d895`, not `20f1efaa`. I amended and force-pushed in response to your review, so the commit you actually read stopped being the branch head and only the fixed version survived under a new SHA. That is what made it look like the bound had been there all along — my fault for amending mid-review rather than adding a follow-up commit.

`e45d895` is orphaned but still retrievable:

```
gh api "repos/c8dhjp4tyv-bit/codex-router/contents/src/reasoning-tag-stripper.mjs?ref=e45d895" --jq '.content' | base64 -d
```

It contains zero occurrences of `MAX_PENDING_LEAD`, and its `#lead` is:

```js
#lead(out) {
  if (this.#emittedVisible) return out;
  if (!out) return "";
  const held = this.#pendingLead + out;
  const trimmed = held.replace(/^\s+/, "");
  if (!trimmed) {
    this.#pendingLead = held;
    return "";
  }
  ...
}
```

Both halves of what you raised, exactly as you described them: the accumulation re-trimmed on every delta, and no ceiling on it.

It also measured the way you predicted. Whitespace-only deltas of 64 characters, driven through the real `ReasoningTagStripper`:

| deltas | `e45d895` | merged |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2,000 | 160 ms | 53 ms |
| 8,000 | 4,088 ms | 81 ms |
| 16,000 | 14,398 ms | 114 ms |

1 MB held at the end of the last one, versus nothing.

The method comment you quoted back at me is one I wrote *while* implementing the fix you asked for — it describes the bound, it didn't precede it. I hadn't anticipated the critique.

One more correction while I'm at it: my first version of the complexity guard used 313 deltas and passed on the unbounded code too, so it guarded nothing. I caught it only by running it against the pre-fix tree. The merged one is sized from the table above — ~26x headroom on the bounded implementation, ~5x overshoot on the old one.

