```js
abort({ hard = false } = {}) {
  if (this.closed) return;
  this.closed = true;
  this.parser.stop();
  this.abortController.abort(new Error("Responses WebSocket closed."));
  this.continuations.clear();
  if (this.socket.destroyed) return;
  if (hard) this.socket.destroy();
  else this.socket.destroySoon();
}
```

with the three transport listeners in `start()` calling `this.abort({ hard: true })`. Without it, an upgraded socket whose peer drops without a close frame is never released (405 leaked descriptors on a router up for two days). The regression test is `releases the upgraded socket when the peer drops without a close frame` in `test/responses-websocket.test.mjs`. The new upstream client in this PR owns sockets too, so the same rule is worth checking there.

